From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 22 Jan 94 14:23:47 EST
Subject: [#94-1-413] EXTRO-1: Dogma Police

Max More wrote: <I am all in favor of a session at Extro 1 to generate means of
warding off encrustations of dogma.  If this area seems important to you, and
you have some ideas, please let me know if you would like to sit on a panel at
the conference.>

I would be happy to sit on this panel.  I would even sit in between Geoff Dale
and Romana Machado so they could have clear shots at me (only if they are
interested, of course).  I have recently been thinking about conceptual fields
dotted with clustered attractors (energy wells).  If individual beliefs are like
marbles rolling along the field, the path of the marbles curve around wells and
may be captured.  The deeper the well, the more energy it takes to move out of
it (like dogma); the more shallow the well, the more easily skirted.  (I know,
it sounds like pictures of relativistic gravity but metaphors are only good if
they are familiar).  There are complex relationships between the width of the
attractor at the top vs. the depth of it; the degree of clustering around a
central attractor; and the fitness, or closeness of match, of the conceptual
attractors to the underlying objective reality (through induction or deduction).
If your beliefs never settle into any attractors as they roll along, you are in
danger of losing your marbles.

Max wrote: <I think we see that in the workings of the state-science system
today, where established views use the mantle of science to crush alternative 
approaches.>

I have had bad experiences with state-science oppressiveness in my children's
schools.  My oldest son wants to be a genetic engineer and faces constant
victimization because "we shouldn't tamper with nature."  It depresses him and
makes me angry.  I'm all for a healthy dose of nomad-science.

Max: <By contrast I advocate alliances with other groups, transhumanist or not,
on issues where we share values and goals.>

Issue by issue.  Ad hoc.

