Mike Price responds:
>
>Steve Whitrow writes:
>
>> The main threats to Britain's traditional liberties are the
>> Major Government and the European Commission.
>
>The Conservatives may not be perfect but they're a lot better than the
>most probable alternative - the Labour Party... <shudder>.

Labour could well be worse, but the difference between the parties has
narrowed quite a bit in the last decade.  As libertarians or anarcho-
capitalists, we've got about three possible voting strategies:

(i)  Vote for the party with the best rhetoric (e.g. Conservative /
Republican).  Trouble is, rhetoric doesn't always match reality, and
promises to "roll back the frontiers of the state" tend to mean more
laws, increased controls, and higher taxes.

(ii)  Refuse to vote.  (From Tom Morrow's analysis of over a year ago,
on how the costs of voting, time involved etc, outweigh the expected
benefits of voting, taking into account the likelihood of an individual
vote making a difference.)  This also allows you to express non-
recognition of the legitimacy of something which, by personal
aesthetics, is immoral.  

(iii)  Always vote against the incumbents (suggested by someone in a
comparatively recent post), to keep government as weak as possible.
This also partly satisfies a desire for revenge!

I think all of these have merit, and haven't decided which to operate
at the next election.

I find the triangle / pyramid model, with ascension corresponding to
increasing libertarianism, much better than the 1-D left-right line.
There just isn't a suitable gap to fit anarcho-capitalism into between
fascism and "dry" Conservatism.  (Although what could depth represent
-- isn't the third dimension just a gimmick?)  The economically dry
believe in "moral values", the "importance" of religion etc, even more
strongly than the wets.  On the triangle model, the Conservatives are
down on the bottom line along with Labour, National Socialism, etc, all
equidistant heightwise from the libertarian top section.

