From: "Harry S. Hawk" <habs@panix.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Jan 1994 15:11:36 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-1-416] Meta: Bounced Mail Charges

This is an attempt to clarify the following statement in my recent
Admin: post as several list members have written me with concern.
They are concerned because almost every account bounces from time to
time, bounces being a normal part of "life" on the internet.

We have built $$ into our pricing model for a nominal amount of
bounces for the list as a whole. This policy is not aimed at that type
of bounce.

I wrote:
> Legal Stuff: We will attempt to deliver 1 copy of all mail. We are not
> ... If your mail starts bouncing you will be removed from the list,
> until YOU notify us to attempt delivery again. If your mail bounces
> often, you will be asked to pay $10 to cover the extra bandwidth
> charges and administrative overhead (we pay for incoming and outgoing
> messages and a single user's single day's worth of bounced messages
> can total 500 messages or more; that's about 500k).

0) This doesn't apply to the odd bounce - every account bounces from
time to time. (see above)

1) This doesn't apply to well known services and sites going down.
(e.g., Netcom's mail machine was down once or twice this year).

2) In the past when I noticed an account bouncing for more than a few
days I would delete it from the list, unless it was an account of a
frequent poster (I don't read every post - so I am talking about
posters known to me.) After a few more days I will then delete those
list members also. Most often after deleting an account I NEVER heard
from that person/site again. About ~ 5-10% of the time, I would get a
message asking what happened, and MOST of the time, the message said
something like: you probably deleted my account as my system was down
for the last 4 days .... please add me back. I have always done so,
and normally that site never bounces again.. I will continue with
this policy.

3) In the past when we were not charging for the list, I don't think
people always cared about getting posts; now that we are charging I assume
every user wants every post; which is why we increased the "resend" cache
to 1 week. I assume with our pay-for-membership, if someone's account
is bouncing they will quickly move to fix it, even if that means moving
to an other service provider.

4) However, there are a few accounts that bounce almost all the time.
That doesn't mean every message bounces but I do get bounced messages
from them almost everyday. That is because many systems send 3 to 6 or
more messages, one or more per day, (containing the entire original
post) letting me know the message is queued waiting for delivery... We
have calculated this type of bounce costs us $7+ in bandwidth per year.
We charge $10 to cover the admin costs (like me deleting 1000's of
message from my inbox.) These are accounts my "charge for bounces"
policy is aimed at.

5) The accounts that bounce the most are often SLIP and PPP users who
get the list sent directly to their personal machine. Other accounts
that bounce like this have the list sent to their machine which is
connected to the Internet via some corporate "net." Often these users
turn of their machine for the weekend, or for other periods of time.
That means, although they might ultimately get the mail, I get lots
and lots of bounces, which I have to sort, filter, and/or delete, and
this costs ExI extra bandwidth charges also.

I hope this explains things in more detail. I trust that everyone
understands why I don't wish to increase everyone's list fee to pay
for the few users who bounce this way.

Again, you don't need to worry if your mail bounces from time to time,
even if it occurs with some frequency. 

Again, if something happens to your site and your mail bounces enough
to have your account removed, you still don't have to worry, this
policy is not aimed at you. You will be put back on the list at no
additional cost.

You do have to worry if you account bounces day after day, week after
week. If this happens to you, you are probably experiencing problems
getting other mail - you should consider getting a more reliable
connection to the net. If you don't wish to get a better net
connection, we will have to charge you the $10. We don't make any
profit on this but we don't feel we should not have to pay for your
"poor" connection either. If you are charged with this, you will have
been given at least one warning to solve you mail delivery problems or
you will be charged for the extra bandwidth.

If this message don't answer your questions about this policy, please
write me privately. I will seek to answer your questions; after it is
concluded you can summarize our "conversation" to the list if you feel
it is warranted.


/hawk

