From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Fri, 21 Jan 94 20:37:19 EST
Subject: [#94-1-425] Extropianism is *not* a religion.

Max More says-

> I will always welcome
> *reasonable* and contentful feedback on the Extropian Principles and on the
> question of how to avoid creeping dogma in our movement.

Max, your post contains a lot to think about, but here are a couple ideas:

Who or what defines the principles, and defines who is extropian?
Notice I'm not asking what *are* the principles and who is an extropian,
but what person, group or process defines these things?  (Btw, it is
problematical at best to try to answer this question within the 
principles.  More on that below.)  One way to approach this question is,
what purposes do defining and refering to these things have for what 
people?  Any time Max or anyone else talks about the principles or 
extropians, some cloud of an answer to these questions is implied.  
Notice the misimpression given by Geoff when he quoted Max to Tim.

It's problematical to quote the principles themselves on the topic of
non-dogmatism, because this is effectively calling them as character
witnesses for themselves.  In fact, if the principles talk too loudly
about non-dogmatism, there's a "protest too much" problem.  Anyway,
the real issue is what happens around these ideas in practice.

"Operating system for ourselves" *is* a problem.  For one thing, a real 
operating system takes over a machine completely; there's nothing that
happens that isn't supposed to happen according to the operating
system.  A guiding philosophy is (appropriately) a much weaker thing.

The other problem is the word "ourselves," and any use of "us" or 
"we."  If you define "us" tightly, then that implies that people who
don't fit the tight definition aren't extropians.  But if you define
it loosely, *and* make statements about "us," then you're putting
words in our mouths, implying that we agree with you or that you know
us or what's right for us!  I don't know how to deal with this paradox 
except "very carefully."  

> ...Talk of "Extropian music", "Extropian diet", etc., are 
> (or should be, in my view) shorthand for "music likely to be enjoyed by 
> extropically-minded folks, or music that invigorates and energizes" 
                                           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> and "diets that contribute towards values shared by those who think 
> of themselves as Extropian" -- i.e., health, longevity, enjoyment.
                                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^  
> This does not mean (as in Rand's writing) that certain musicians 
> must be liked by all Extropians (responses to music are personaland 
> determined by many hard to define factors; I like techno music for its energy
> and high-tech sound, other highly extropian people don't get much out of it).
> Some music could plausibly be called non-extropian (small "e") or even 
> anti-extropian, such as music that glorifies mindless destruction, despair,
                                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> hatred, or which attacks civilization, freedom, and advancement.  So, there is
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> no mandatory "Extropian music", either in general style nor in particular
> artists.  We can only draw very general and fuzzy lines around what we want to
> regard as extropian music....

Those terms are too vague and too close to "good" and "bad".  Talking that way
seems to be either using "extropian" as a synonym for "good," or baldly claiming 
that extropian ideas lead to good things, or good taste in things, none of
which implications help make extropianism criticizable.

I think the larger problem is that extropianism has to have a way of
distinguishing itself from good ideas in general.  There have to be things
that make something an extropian idea rather than just a good idea or
the term loses meaning.

But of course if you narrow it too much, it loses appeal!

The problems of making strong ideas that don't turn into ideologies,
or good interest groups that don't turn into cults, are tough, fun and
worthwhile.  I think even the minds of rationalists are seeded with memes
that steer toward these dead ends.  Cults and ideologies are like madness 
and undirected rebellion--they serve as self-made quarantines against 
change.

